WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:01.866
This code shows how one class can

00:00:01.892 --> 00:00:05.132
create two separate objects We define a

00:00:05.145 --> 00:00:08.421
class named Product The dunder init method

00:00:08.447 --> 00:00:09.908
receives a name and a price and

00:00:09.934 --> 00:00:11.526
stores them as self dot name and

00:00:11.553 --> 00:00:14.868
self dot price The class also contains

00:00:14.895 --> 00:00:17.829
a method named sale price A method

00:00:17.855 --> 00:00:19.671
is simply a function that belongs to

00:00:19.697 --> 00:00:22.895
a class The sale price method receives

00:00:22.921 --> 00:00:26.145
a discount rate It multiplies the object's

00:00:26.158 --> 00:00:28.316
price by one minus the discount rate

00:00:29.237 --> 00:00:32.658
Next we create item one Item one

00:00:32.671 --> 00:00:35.079
is a Product object representing a laptop

00:00:35.105 --> 00:00:36.868
with a regular price of nine hundred

00:00:36.895 --> 00:00:41.276
dollars Then we create item two Item

00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:43.513
two represents a monitor with a regular

00:00:43.526 --> 00:00:46.763
price of three hundred dollars Notice that

00:00:46.789 --> 00:00:49.079
both objects use the same Product class

00:00:49.316 --> 00:00:52.303
but they store different values The print

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:55.000
statements display each product's regular price and

00:00:55.026 --> 00:00:56.882
its sale price using a ten percent

00:00:56.895 --> 00:01:00.197
discount Before running the code let us

00:01:00.224 --> 00:01:03.210
predict the results Ten percent of nine

00:01:03.237 --> 00:01:05.461
hundred is ninety so the laptop sale

00:01:05.474 --> 00:01:07.447
price should be eight hundred ten dollars

00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:10.210
Ten percent of three hundred is thirty

00:01:10.447 --> 00:01:12.079
so the monitor sale price should be

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:15.408
two hundred seventy dollars Click Run now

00:01:15.421 --> 00:01:17.566
and confirm those results in the terminal

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.105
Next try creating a third object For

00:01:22.118 --> 00:01:24.632
example create a Product named Keyboard with

00:01:24.658 --> 00:01:27.382
a price of one hundred Then print

00:01:27.408 --> 00:01:29.184
its sale price using the same ten

00:01:29.197 --> 00:01:32.553
percent discount This demonstrates one of the

00:01:32.579 --> 00:01:36.763
central ideas of object-oriented programming We
define

00:01:36.789 --> 00:01:38.500
the behavior once in the class and

00:01:38.526 --> 00:01:40.355
reuse it across many objects
